The Significance Of The European Union As Global Actor
Economic Influence
The EU Doesn’t Have Major Economic Influence and Hasn’t Been a Success for Its
Member States:
EU Member States Aren’t Able to Protect Their Own Economies and Have Limited Economic
Sovereignty:
● One of the fundamental criticisms of the EU’s economic structure is that it limits member
states’ ability to protect their own economies, as they are subject to EU-wide policies and
trade agreements that may not align with individual economic priorities.
○ By pooling authority over trade and competition policies, EU member states
sacrifice a degree of economic sovereignty, leaving them unable to fully control
tariffs, subsidies, or other protections for domestic industries.
○ For example, under EU competition law, countries are generally prohibited from
providing state aid to industries that may need support, such as steel or agriculture,
without prior approval from the European Commission.
● This restriction can hurt countries with struggling sectors, such as Italy which has struggling
automotive and steel industries and may have wished to support them in the context of
global competition to aid their competitiveness and prevent job losses. However, they are
unable to due to EU policies.
● The lack of control over trade and economic policies means that EU member states face
challenges in addressing local economic needs and protecting industries in times of crisis.
The European Union is Increasingly Caught Between and Unable to Compete with China and
the United States:
● The EU’s economic power is increasingly challenged on the global stage, as it is caught
between two dominant economic powers: China and the United States.
● Unlike the US and China, which have centralised control over their economic policies, the
EU’s economic power is constrained by internal divisions and a lack of unified strategic
direction. This structural limitation makes it difficult for the EU to respond quickly and
effectively to global economic shifts.
○ Recently, both China (Made In China 2025) and the United States (Inflation
Reduction Act) have implemented state-driven industrial strategies to compete in
critical sectors such as green technology and semiconductors, however the EU isn’t
able to because it faces restrictions on state aid and doesn’t have a unified
industrial policy pooled between member states.
○ Germany in particular is struggling as a manufacturing powerhouse with an
important automotive sector that is being outcompeted by China when it comes to
electric vehicles.
● Additionally, when the US initiated a trade war with China in 2018, the EU struggled to
respond effectively to shifts in global supply chains, and its businesses faced disruptions
from US tariffs.
○ Member states reliant on exports to the US or China, such as Germany and Italy,
often find themselves economically vulnerable to policy changes by these larger
powers, highlighting the EU’s limited capacity to protect its members in a global
economy dominated by the US-China rivalry.
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 3
Key Recent Example - The European Union Caught Up In The U.S.-China Trade War:
● As the U.S.-China trade war escalated rapidly in April 2025 early on in Trump's second term
as President, the EU found itself largely sidelined.
○ Despite being a major economic player, it wasn’t exempt from the U.S. tariffs,
receiving a 10% tariff on all its exports to the U.S. (lowered from 20% a couple of
weeks earlier).
○ Further, it struggled to have its voice heard to intervene in the major economic
conflict that threatened the global economy and free trade, which is a key principle
supported by the EU.
● This is therefore a telling example of how the EU is struggling within a global economy and
world order that is increasingly being defined by bipolar competition between the U.S. and
China. It is economically vulnerable and lacks leverage, whilst potentially also struggling to
have a voice/impact due to being formed of a number of states with different economies,
outlooks and interests.
The EU Benefits Wealthier Member States More Than Poorer Member States:
● Economic benefits within the EU are unevenly distributed, often favouring wealthier
member states over poorer ones, which exacerbates economic inequality across the Union.
● Wealthier countries like Germany and the Netherlands benefit greatly from the EU’s single
market and customs union, using their strong export industries to tap into vast EU markets
without barriers.
○ Germany, for example, exports over 50% of its goods to other EU countries, taking
full advantage of the customs-free single market.
● In contrast, less affluent member states, particularly in Southern and Eastern Europe, often
struggle to compete within the single market.
○ In 2022, the unemployment rate in Greece and Spain was 12.6% and 13.3%
respectively, compared to the EU average of 6%, illustrating the persistent economic
challenges faced by some Southern European economies within the single market.
● The 2008 eurozone crisis further highlighted these inequalities, exposing structural flaws in
the euro that made it difficult for economically weaker nations, such as Greece, Spain, and
Portugal, to recover.
○ Greece, in particular, faced stringent austerity measures imposed by the EU and the
International Monetary Fund (IMF) as a condition for bailout packages, leading to
long-term economic hardship, including a 25% decrease in GDP and youth
unemployment rates exceeding 50% at the height of the crisis.
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 4
○ At the same time, countries like Germany emerged from the crisis relatively
unscathed, poorer economies endured years of economic stagnation, high
unemployment, and reduced public services. This disparity has led to frustration
among citizens of less affluent EU countries, who see the EU as prioritising the
interests of wealthier states.
The EU Has Major Economic Influence and Has Been a Success for Its Member
States:
The EU’s Huge Single Market and Customs Union Allows it to Negotiate Favourable Trade
Deals and Improve the Economies of its Member States:
● The European Union’s single market, representing over 15% of global GDP, enables the EU
to secure favourable trade agreements that support economic growth across its member
states. Acting as a unified bloc, the EU negotiates on behalf of its 27 members, which
collectively allows it to secure trade deals that smaller or medium-sized states would
struggle to achieve alone.
○ For instance, the EU-Canada Comprehensive Economic and Trade Agreement
(CETA) eliminated tariffs on 98% of goods traded between the two regions,
benefiting EU exporters and reducing costs for businesses.
○ Similarly, the EU-South Korea Free Trade Agreement has increased EU exports to
South Korea by 76% since its implementation, benefiting member states with strong
export sectors, such as Germany in machinery and Ireland in pharmaceuticals.
○ The EU is a major economic power and is the largest trading partner for over 80
countries, which gives it significant leverage.
● These agreements bolster GDP and support jobs, with estimates indicating that over 36
million jobs in the EU are directly dependent on exports to non-EU countries.
○ For countries like Portugal and the Czech Republic, which rely on export markets for
economic growth, these trade agreements provide critical economic opportunities,
showcasing the success of the EU’s single market in supporting a wide range of
member state economies.
● The customs union, which eliminates internal tariffs, also provides seamless access for EU
businesses to a market of over 450 million consumers, further strengthening member
states’ economies.
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 5
The EU’s Economic Might Allows It to Compete with China and the US:
● On the other hand, it can be argued that the EU’s economic power enables its member
states to compete effectively on the global stage with major economies like China and the
US, granting a collective strength that individual countries wouldn’t have alone.
○ For example, in October 2024 the EU protected its member states’ automotive
industries by imposing additional tariffs on Chinese electric vehicles (EVs), with
rates ranging from 7.8% to 35.3%, citing concerns over unfair subsidies provided to
Chinese manufacturers.
○ Similarly, in response to China’s alleged dumping of steel, the EU imposed tariffs on
Chinese steel products to protect European producers in 2016, supporting member
states like Italy and Poland, which have significant steel industries.
● As the world’s second-largest economic bloc, the EU’s size also allows it to set global
standards in industries ranging from environmental regulation to digital privacy, influencing
production and regulatory practices worldwide.
○ The EU’s General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) has had a particularly
notable impact, with countries like Japan and Brazil adopting similar privacy laws in
response to the EU’s lead.
● Additionally, in the renewable energy sector, the EU’s aggressive carbon reduction targets
and green technology investments have made it a global leader, with member states like
Germany and Denmark leading in wind and solar energy innovations.
○ These efforts help EU businesses compete globally, with EU renewable energy
exports worth over €72 billion in 2020.
The EU is Able to Use its Economic Leverage to Further Democracy and Human Rights:
● The EU also uses itseconomic influence to promote democracy, human rights, and stability,
both within its borders and in the rest of the world.
● The EU and its member states collectively account for over half of global development aid,
with the EU itself providing around €80 billion annually.
○ This aid is often tied to conditions related to human rights and governance, such as
with the EU’s support for African nations through the European Development Fund
(EDF), where funds are linked to improvements in governance and transparency.
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 6
● EU membership requirements also further these values, as potential members must meet
the Copenhagen Criteria, which include commitments to democracy, human rights, and the
rule of law. Potential member states meet these commitments due to the economic benefits
that EU membership would have to their countries
○ This has been instrumental in transforming political systems across Central and
Eastern Europe. For instance, Poland and Hungary enacted sweeping judicial and
political reforms to qualify for EU membership, increasing political stability and
aligning with democratic norms.
○ The EU also uses its economic leverage to support this through its Instrument for
Pre-Accession Assistance (IPA) which has provided over €12 billion to candidate
countries, including Serbia and Montenegro, to support political and economic
reforms that align with EU standards.
Geopolitical/Military Influence
The EU Is a Major Geopolitical and Military Power
The EU Has Often Coordinated Its Foreign and Security Policies to Have a Significant Impact:
● The EU’s collective foreign and security policies enable it to act as a unified force on critical
global issues, leveraging the combined political and economic weight of its 27 member
states. This coordination has allowed the EU to take decisive actions that would be
challenging for individual nations.
● One prominent example is the EU’s unified response to Russia’s invasion of Ukraine, where
it imposed multiple rounds of sanctions targeting Russia’s financial system, energy exports,
and key industries, cutting off critical funding sources for Moscow.
○ These sanctions included freezing Russian central bank assets and banning
transactions with major Russian banks through the SWIFT international payments
system.
○ This economic pressure, combined with EU support for Ukraine in the form of
military aid, humanitarian assistance, and refuge for millions of displaced
Ukrainians, underscores the EU’s capacity to project geopolitical influence through
collective action.
● Additionally, the EU played a key role in brokering the Joint Comprehensive Plan of Action
(JCPOA) with Iran, working alongside the United States, China, and Russia to negotiate an
agreement that limited Iran’s nuclear activities in exchange for lifting economic sanctions.
This shows how the EU can act as a major geopolitical power on par with the world’s most
powerful countries.
● EU leaders, notably French President Emmanuel Macron, have long advocated for
“strategic autonomy,” pushing for an EU that speaks with its own voice and reduces
dependency on the US. Macron has argued that the EU needs to “avoid being followers”
and build an independent geopolitical strategy, highlighting the ambition within Europe to
develop a distinctive, impactful presence on the global stage.
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 7
The EU Is Increasing Its Defence Capabilities and Peacekeeping Role:
● The EU has taken significant steps to develop its defence and peacekeeping capacities,
evolving from an economic union into a body with real military ambitions.
● Through the Common Security and Defence Policy (CSDP), the EU has conducted over 30
peacekeeping and stabilisation missions worldwide, including military training operations in
Mali, counter-piracy efforts off the Horn of Africa, and monitoring missions in Bosnia and
Herzegovina.
○ These missions demonstrate the EU’s commitment to supporting global stability,
even in regions far from its own borders.
● The EU’s strategic focus has intensified with the launch of Permanent Structured
Cooperation (PESCO) in 2017, which facilitates deeper defence cooperation among 25 EU
member states, fostering joint projects such as new military technologies, cyber defence
initiatives, and coordinated rapid response units.
○ Additionally, the European Defence Fund (EDF), established with a budget of €8
billion, supports collaborative research and development in defence technology, with
the goal of making Europe’s defence sector more competitive and innovative.
● The 2022 Russian invasion of Ukraine has prompted the EU to accelerate its push toward a
stronger, more autonomous defence posture, with calls for increased defence spending and
greater investment in European defence capabilities.
○ Some leaders, including Germany’s Chancellor Olaf Scholz, have proposed the idea
of a “European army” to ensure that the EU can independently protect its interests
and contribute meaningfully to global security.
The EU Has Significant Soft Power:
● The EU wields extensive soft power, positioning itself as a global leader in promoting
democracy, human rights, and sustainable development.
○ With the US stepping back from global engagement under the Trump administration,
the EU stepped forward to advocate for liberal values and multilateral cooperation,
further establishing itself as a champion of democracy and human rights on the
world stage.
○ For example, during the Trump administration’s withdrawal from the Paris Climate
Agreement in 2017, the EU reaffirmed its commitment to the accord and increased
its climate leadership, working closely with China and other global partners to
advance climate action.
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 8
● The EU and its member states collectively account for over 50% of global development aid,
providing approximately €80 billion annually. This assistance is strategically allocated to
support economic development, poverty alleviation, and governance reforms, especially in
Africa, the Middle East, and the EU’s eastern neighbourhood.
○ For example, through the European Development Fund (EDF), the EU has provided
billions of euros to Sub-Saharan Africa to improve education, healthcare, and
infrastructure, often tying aid to commitments on human rights and good
governance.
● The EU also has an extensive diplomatic network, with over 140 delegations worldwide,
which allows it to engage in multilateral diplomacy, mediate conflicts, and promote its
strategic interests globally.
The EU Isn’t a Major Geopolitical and Military Power
The EU Lacks a Centralised Military Force and Is Dependent on NATO for Security and
Defence:
● A significant constraint on the EU’s ability to operate as an independent security power is its
lack of a unified, centralised military force and its consequent reliance on NATO and the US
in particular.
○ While the EU has developed cooperative defence mechanisms, such as the
Common Security and Defence Policy (CSDP), these initiatives lack the rapid
deployment capabilities and firepower required to respond effectively to major
security crises and there is no unified EU army.
○ The EU’s inability to independently deploy military power limits its credibility as a
global security actor and restricts its capacity to respond unilaterally to threats within
and beyond its borders.
● In the 1990s, during the wars in Bosnia and Kosovo, the EU was unable to mobilise a
unified military response to prevent ethnic violence and humanitarian atrocities.
○ Instead, NATO, heavily led by US forces, intervened with operations like Operation
Deliberate Force and Operation Allied Force to stabilise the region.
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 9
● More recently, with the 2022 Russian invasion of Ukraine, the EU has played a supportive
role by imposing sanctions and providing economic and humanitarian aid, but it remains
dependent on NATO for the deterrence and defence of Eastern Europe.
Key Recent Example - The European Union Sidelined In Relation To Ukraine:
● After Trump’s return to the Presidency in 2025, the European Union found itself completely
sidelined in discussions about the Ukraine war and potential peace negotiations, despite
the fact that EU countries were major financial supporters of Ukraine along with the U.S.
● Trump pushed strongly for a ceasefire and swift peace negotiations, even though this is
likely to result in an agreement that is more favourable to Russia.
○ These peace negotiations were held between the U.S., Russia and Ukraine,
however no EU leaders were invited.
● Further, the EU also found itself sidelined as it was unable to offer a credible military
guarantee to Ukraine when the U.S. refused to do so, due to its collective lack of military
strength because of decades of underinvestment.
● Together, this demonstrates how the EU’s military weakness has left it with limited
geopolitical influence, even in questions about Europe that are strongly consequential for its
security. This has meant that it continues to be very dependent on the U.S.
Divisions Between Member States Prevent the EU From Having a Unified Stance on Global
Issues:
● The EU’s internal divisions often prevent it from presenting a cohesive foreign policy,
weakening its geopolitical impact. Member states have diverse interests and priorities in
foreign policy, which can lead to stark disagreements on how to approach global issues.
○ For example, during the US-led War on Terror, EU member states took opposing
stances: the UK and Poland joined the US-led coalition in Iraq, while Germany and
France strongly opposed the intervention.
● These internal divisions have also appeared in responses to crises such as the 2015
migrant crisis, when member states disagreed on how to distribute refugees across the EU,
with countries like Germany initially opening their borders, while others, such as Hungary,
constructed border fences and rejected EU-mandated quotas.
● In recent years, similar divisions have arisen regarding relations with China, with some
countries, like Italy, joining China’s Belt and Road Initiative for economic gains, while
others, including France, advocate a tougher stance due to concerns over human rights.
○ These internal rifts prevent the EU from establishing a strong, unified voice on the
global stage, making it difficult to adopt consistent policies and reducing its
effectiveness as a geopolitical actor.
The EU Lacks Hard Power and Is Therefore Much Less Powerful Than the US or China:
● The EU’s limited hard power capabilities place it at a disadvantage in the global power
hierarchy, particularly in the context of strategic competition between the US and China.
● Unlike the United States, which has the world’s most powerful military, or China, which is
expanding its military presence globally, the EU lacks the independent military strength to
project power beyond its borders.
○ This limitation is especially apparent in high-stakes geopolitical areas, like the South
China Sea, where the EU’s diplomatic appeals are largely overshadowed by US and
Chinese military manoeuvres.
● The EU’s efforts to respond to the US-China rivalry have been hampered by internal
debates on whether to align more closely with Washington or pursue a more balanced
stance.
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 10
○ France and Germany have expressed concerns about becoming overly dependent
on the US, with French President Macron calling for “strategic autonomy.” However,
Eastern European countries, particularly those bordering Russia, generally support
closer alignment with the US, fearing Russian aggression.
○ This indecision has left the EU struggling to carve out a coherent geopolitical
position, and its lack of hard power has led major powers to overlook the EU in
strategic decisions, such as in Asia-Pacific security arrangements.
● The EU’s absence of military force and hesitancy in defining a firm stance in the US-China
competition have weakened its influence, leading to perceptions that it is a secondary
player in global power dynamics.
Structural Power
The EU Has Significant Structural Power
The EU Has Significant Structural Power in Trade and Regulation:
● The EU’s structural power in trade and regulation makes it one of the most influential
players in the global economy, where it often acts as a “regulatory superpower.” As the
world’s largest single market, the EU leverages its economic size to set global standards in
areas such as environmental protection, consumer safety, and data privacy.
● This phenomenon, often referred to as the “Brussels Effect,” occurs when multinational
companies and even other countries adapt their practices to comply with EU regulations to
access its market of over 450 million consumers.
○ A prime example is the EU’s General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR), which
has set an international standard for data privacy. Countries including Japan, Brazil,
and South Korea have adopted similar data privacy laws in response, illustrating the
EU’s far-reaching regulatory influence.
● Additionally, the EU plays a central role in the World Trade Organization (WTO), where its
collective weight allows it to negotiate and advocate for fair trade practices and high
standards.
○ In the WTO, the EU led efforts to ban hormone-treated beef, successfully
advocating for stricter food safety standards that influenced global agricultural
practices.
The EU Plays a Significant Role in a Number of International Governmental Organisations:
● The EU’s active participation in major international governmental organisations, such as the
G7, G20, International Monetary Fund (IMF), and World Health Organization (WHO),
enhances its structural power on the world stage.
○ Within these institutions, the EU uses its economic clout to influence policies on
issues ranging from financial stability to global health and climate action.
○ As a prominent G7 and G20 member, the EU represents its collective economic
interests, working alongside other global powers to shape responses to crises such
as the COVID-19 pandemic and the 2008 financial crisis.
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 11
● Additionally, the EU’s influence extends within its borders, where it holds substantial
structural power over its own member states. Through EU laws and directives, member
states are bound by EU standards and policies.
○ The European Court of Justice (ECJ) enforces compliance, ensuring that member
states align with EU rules, which enhances the Union’s structural power and
promotes a cohesive policy framework across Europe.
The EU Has Limited Structural Power
It Is Dependent on Its Member States to Make and Enforce Decisions
● The EU’s structural power is limited by its dependence on member states for both
decision-making and enforcement, particularly in areas where national sovereignty and
identity play significant roles.
○ While the EU can create policies, its implementation relies heavily on member
states, leading to inconsistencies in enforcement, especially in areas like
environmental standards and fiscal policies.
● Tensions over sovereignty have intensified in foreign and defence policy, where member
states hold divergent views; for instance, while some countries advocate for increased EU
defence autonomy, others, such as Poland and the Baltic states, are reluctant to reduce
reliance on NATO, which leads to a lack of unified action.
○ During the War on Terror, when the US told the UN that it intended to invade Iraq,
EU countries in the Security Council were divided, with the UK supporting the
invasion and France opposing it.
● Additionally, in the face of geopolitical challenges like migration and relations with major
powers such as Russia and China, these internal divisions limit the EU’s capacity to act
cohesively, diminishing its structural power and making it heavily dependent on member
state cooperation.
It Has Limited Influence in Some International Governmental Organisations (IGOs):
● The EU’s structural power is further constrained by its limited influence in some key
international governmental organisations, particularly in defence and security structures,
which are critical for asserting geopolitical influence.
● While the EU participates in the United Nations (UN) and has observer status in some UN
bodies, it lacks a permanent seat on the UN Security Council, where major decisions on
international security are made.
● In terms of defence, the EU’s reliance on NATO means that it has limited capacity to act
independently in global security matters, as it must depend on the alliance, and ultimately
the US, for military action.
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 12
Key Recent Example - The EU’s Commitment To A Rules-Based Order That No Longer
Exists:
● The world order and global economy in 2025 is increasingly being defined by major powers
ignoring the rules of the post-Cold War international order and instead simply acting in their
own national interests, including through the use of hard power.
○ International Governmental Organisations like the WTO and ICJ are being
ignored/being non-functional in the case of the WTO, whilst global trade rules and
international law are being openly flouted by the U.S. and China.
● The EU remains the only major actor that is still strongly committed to the rules of the
international order, including supporting multilateral institutions, international law and global
cooperation.
○ In many ways, this has left the EU isolated, as the U.S. and China have benefitted
themselves by ignoring these principles.
● This is therefore a useful way in which the EU can be seen as having limited power in the
current world order.
The Extent To Which State Sovereignty Has Been Eroded By
The European Union
EU Membership Does Erode State Sovereignty
The Supremacy of EU Law Over National Law
● The principle of EU law supremacy means that, in cases of conflict, EU law takes
precedence over national legislation, significantly limiting member states’ control over policy
areas that the EU controls.
○ This principle was firmly established in the Costa v ENEL (1964) ruling by the
European Court of Justice (ECJ), which declared that EU law is supreme and
binding on all member states.
● This supremacy affects various policy areas, including competition law, trade,
environmental standards, and consumer protection, where EU regulations must be adopted
even if they contradict national preferences.
○ For instance, EU environmental directives set binding targets for emissions and
renewable energy that member states must meet, restricting their flexibility to
develop their own environmental policies, even though there may be domestic
political pressures to do so.
○ Similarly, the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR), which governs data
privacy, applies uniformly across the EU, preventing member states from creating
less restrictive data policies even if it aligns better with national priorities.
● This supremacy of EU law, enforced by the ECJ, reduces member states’ legislative
autonomy, requiring them to align with EU standards in areas critical to national economies
and social policies, which would otherwise fall under sovereign control.
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 13
Key Supranational Institutions Limit Member States’ Sovereignty
● The EU’s powerful supranational institutions - the European Central Bank (ECB), European
Commission, and European Parliament - impose further limitations on member states’
sovereignty by centralising control in bodies where no individual member state can veto a
decision they disagree with.
● The ECB, for example, dictates monetary policy for the 20 Eurozone countries, setting
interest rates and managing inflation. This removes a critical tool of economic control from
national governments, as countries like Italy and Greece cannot independently adjust their
monetary policy to meet specific economic needs.
● The European Commission also has extensive authority to propose and enforce legislation
across the Union, often taking action against member states that violate EU rules.
○ For instance, Poland and Hungary have faced sanctions and funding cuts from the
European Commission over alleged rule-of-law violations, highlighting the
Commission’s power to impose penalties on non-compliant member states.
● In the legislative process, the European Parliament—an elected supranational body—also
participates in decision-making that applies across the EU, sometimes advancing policies
that may not reflect all member states’ priorities.
Qualified Majority Voting and the Move Toward Ever Closer Union
● The EU’s commitment to “ever closer union,” stated in foundational treaties like the Treaty
of Rome (1957) and reaffirmed in the Maastricht Treaty (1992), drives a vision of deeper
integration and the further erosion of member states’ sovereignty.
○ Key EU leaders, such as former European Commission President Jean-Claude
Juncker and French President Emmanuel Macron, have advocated for deeper EU
integration and a stronger centralised governance structure, with Macron often
promoting the idea of “strategic autonomy” for Europe.
○ This ideological commitment to “ever closer union” suggests an ongoing shift toward
federalisation, where national sovereignty is continually diluted as more
decision-making power is transferred to EU institutions.
● The commitment to an “ever closer union” can be seen in the increasing use of Qualified
Majority Voting since the EU was founded, which undermines member states’ sovereignty
as they can be forced to accept decisions they disagree with.
https://www.politicsexplained.co.uk/ 14
○ QMV enables EU legislation to be passed by a ‘double majority’ in the Council of the
European Union rather than requiring unanimity, which means that countries can be
outvoted and cannot veto critical policies affecting their national interests.
■ A double majority requires 55% of member states to vote in favour, whilst
these member states must represent at least 65% of the EU population.
○ Over the years, QMV has been extended to include areas like immigration, trade,
and climate policy, resulting in more decisions where individual states cannot
exercise veto power. The Lisbon Treaty (2009) expanded QMV to over 40 new
areas, including judicial cooperation and energy policy, further reducing member
states’ control over important policy domains.
■ In 2015, under QMV, the EU approved mandatory migrant relocation quotas,
requiring countries like Hungary, Poland, and the Czech Republic to accept a
share of refugees, despite their strong opposition.
■ Poland was overruled in 2020 when the EU set more ambitious climate
targets under QMV, mandating a reduction in carbon emissions by at least
55% by 2030, despite Poland’s concerns about the impact on its
coal-dependent economy.
EU Membership Doesn’t Erode State Sovereignty
Member States Retain Control Over Key Areas of Policy and Can Secure Opt-Outs
● Despite the EU’s influence in various areas, member states maintain full control over critical
policy domains, including national defence, health, education, and taxation, where the EU
can’t decide a policy without agreement from all member states.
○ Each country’s military remains fully under national control, with no EU-led standing
army or compulsory EU military requirements, allowing countries like France and
Poland to conduct independent defence operations.
○ In education, member states set their own curriculums and funding levels,
preserving cultural and educational autonomy.
● Additionally, some member states have negotiated opt-outs from specific EU policies, which
allow them to maintain control in areas they consider sensitive or essential.
○ Denmark, for example, opted out of adopting the euro and continues to use the
Danish krone, preserving its independent monetary policy.
○ Ireland, with its opt-out from parts of the justice and home affairs policy, maintains
greater control over its legal system and immigration policies.
○ These opt-outs allow member states to participate in EU integration selectively,
ensuring that they can safeguard sovereignty in areas they deem crucial,
demonstrating that EU membership does not necessarily override national authority
in fundamental policy areas.
● The principle of subsidiarity further protects national sovereignty by ensuring that decisions
are made at the most local level possible, meaning the EU can only act if objectives cannot
be effectively achieved by individual member states. This principle is designed to limit the
EU’s authority to areas where collective action is genuinely necessary, allowing member
states to retain autonomy in areas where they are best positioned to act independently.
The Most Powerful EU Institutions Are Intergovernmental
● While the EU has several influential supranational institutions, such as the European
Commission and European Parliament, many of its most critical decisions are made within
intergovernmental bodies that preserve the authority of individual member states.
