○ The European Council, comprising the heads of state or government from each
member state, is the primary decision-making body for key EU policies and its
strategic direction, particularly in areas like foreign relations and security.
● For example, during negotiations over EU responses to major crises, such as the 2008
financial crisis or the COVID-19 pandemic, the European Council played a central role in
shaping the Union’s strategy.
○ Decisions within the Council require unanimity in almost all cases, meaning that
every country has a veto in areas that could deeply impact national sovereignty,
such as tax policies or constitutional matters.
● The Council of the European Union (also known as the Council of Ministers) allows
ministers from each member state to participate directly in legislative processes, voting on
policies that affect their national interests.
○ On sensitive issues, such as taxation and defence, decisions in the Council still
require unanimity, meaning that no member state can be forced into policies that
contradict its fundamental interests.
○ This intergovernmental framework ensures that countries like Hungary, which has
vetoed certain foreign policy decisions, and Ireland, which maintains the tax
sovereignty that is so important to its economic success, retain the sovereignty to
shape or block EU policies.
Pooled Sovereignty Enhances the Overall Influence of EU Member States
● Pooling sovereignty within the EU enhances the collective influence of member states,
providing them with a platform to wield significant global influence, which they would lack
on their own.
○ Acting as a bloc, the EU has negotiated major trade agreements that benefit all
member states, such as the EU-Canada Comprehensive Economic and Trade
Agreement (CETA) and the EU-Japan Economic Partnership Agreement.
○ These agreements offer member states access to new markets, strengthened
intellectual property protections, and lower tariffs, enhancing economic growth for
both large economies like Germany and smaller ones like Estonia.
● In global organisations, such as the World Trade Organization (WTO), International
Monetary Fund (IMF), and World Health Organization (WHO), the EU’s collective
bargaining power allows it to influence international standards on climate, digital policy, and
health.The EU has led international efforts on climate action, setting ambitious standards
that influence global policy through agreements like the Paris Climate Accord.
● Smaller member states in particular benefit from the EU’s unified voice, which gives them a
level of influence and protection in global affairs far beyond what they could achieve alone.
○ By pooling sovereignty, the EU allows its members to protect their interests on a
global scale, which enhances national sovereignty by providing a stronger position
in global affairs without forcing individual countries to compromise their foundational
values.
