Member States Can Ultimately Leave the EU
● EU membership is ultimately voluntary, and countries have the sovereign right to leave the
Union if they feel their sovereignty or national interests are compromised, as demonstrated
by the United Kingdom’s decision to withdraw via Brexit.
○ The process, clearly outlined in Article 50 of the Lisbon Treaty, provides a legal
pathway for any member state to exit the EU, preserving each country’s ultimate
authority over its membership.
● Brexit underscores that sovereignty ultimately rests with each nation, as the UK regained
full control over its borders, immigration policies, and trade agreements after leaving the
EU. Additionally, Brexit shows that EU membership is flexible, as member states can
assess their relationship with the Union over time and choose whether to continue based
on their evolving priorities.
